From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 21:50:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: Optionally keep compute work blocks
 in session slots

Each compute command's work block (the first 0x900 bytes of its work
image: register list, start and finalize pointers, stamp and notifier
pointers, the descriptor pointer) is written at every publication and
read by the firmware at ingest, by the start op and by the timestamp ops;
finalize writes into it, and the ingest and the post clean and invalidate
it. It lives in the per-VM work objects, mapped uncached. macOS maps its
command records ASC-cached. The existing switch that caches ours
(g16_fw_cached bit 32) puts the cached class on per-VM objects, which the
1.1019 measurement found slower and whose first unmap at client exit
fails.

Add the load-time parameter g16_compute_work_pool. At 1 two session
objects, one uncached and one ASC-cached, each hold one work block slot
per compute unit index; the hardware reads them at a context-0 lower
address reserved at boot above the render work ranges, so no VM is given
it. Every compute command then writes its block into its unit's slot,
the rest of its image stays in the unit's work page, and the block's
firmware and lower addresses and its ring entry name the slot. Compute
units are claimed device-wide and held until their command retires, so
no two commands in flight share a slot, and the session objects are
never dropped. The runtime switch g16_compute_work_cached chooses the
copy for commands published from then on.

The compute_work: stats line counts the commands written to each copy.
At 0, the default, nothing is allocated and no record changes. The slot
layout is in g16/cwslot.rs with host tests.

Assisted-by: LLM
